CVAT Image annotation tool AWS EKS set up with EFS file system
STEP -1
EC2 BASTION HOST SET UP 
   1.  curl https://raw.githubusercontent.com/helm/helm/master/scripts/get-helm-3 | bash
    2.  sudo apt-get update
    3.  sudo curl "https://awscli.amazonaws.com/awscli-exe-linux-x86_64.zip" -o "awscliv2.zip"
    4 . sudo apt-get install unzip
    5.  sudo unzip awscliv2.zip
    6.  sudo ./aws/install
    7.  aws --version
    8.  curl --silent --location "https://github.com/weaveworks/eksctl/releases/latest/download/eksctl_$(uname -s)_amd64.tar.gz" | tar xz -C /tmp
    9.  sudo mv /tmp/eksctl /usr/local/bin
   10.  eksctl version
   11. curl -LO "https://dl.k8s.io/release/$(curl -L -s https://dl.k8s.io/release/stable.txt)/bin/linux/amd64/kubectl"
   12.  sudo install -o root -g root -m 0755 kubectl /usr/local/bin/kubectl
   13.  kubectl version --client
   14.  aws configure
   15.  eksctl create cluster --name cvat-test-2025 --region ap-south-1 --version 1.34 --nodegroup-name linux-nodes --node-type t3.2xlarge --nodes 3 --node-volume-size 100
NOTE:- An AWS EKS cluster with a capacity of three nodes, using EC2 instance type t3.2xlarge with 100 GB storage 
The EKS cluster role should have the following AWS-managed policies attached:
AmazonEKSClusterPolicy      and     AmazonEKSVPCResourceController
 
STEP -2
Git clone https://github.com/cvat-ai/cvat.git

EFS configuration 
1)	Create iamserviceaccount
eksctl create iamserviceaccount   --name efs-csi-controller-sa   --namespace kube-system   --cluster $cluster_name   --role-name $role_name   --attach-policy-arn arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/service-role/AmazonEFSCSIDriverPolicy   --approve
2)	TRUST_POLICY and Create addon
TRUST_POLICY=$(aws iam get-role --output json --role-name $role_name --query 'Role.AssumeRolePolicyDocument' | \
    sed -e 's/efs-csi-controller-sa/efs-csi-*/' -e 's/StringEquals/StringLike/')

aws iam update-assume-role-policy --role-name $role_name --policy-document "$TRUST_POLICY"

export SERVICE_ACCOUNT_ROLE_ARN=arn:aws:iam::157298730309:role/AmazonEKS_EFS_CSI_DriverRole
eksctl create addon   --name aws-efs-csi-driver   --cluster $cluster_name   --region ap-south-1   --service-account-role-arn ${SERVICE_ACCOUNT_ROLE_ARN}   --force

Create custom storage class 
efs-sc.yaml
-----------------------------
apiVersion: storage.k8s.io/v1
kind: StorageClass
metadata:
  name: efs-sc
  annotations:
    storageclass.kubernetes.io/is-default-class: "true"
provisioner: efs.csi.aws.com
parameters:
  provisioningMode: efs-ap
  fileSystemId: fs-02945ccfd93dae06a
  directoryPerms: "700"
reclaimPolicy: Retain
volumeBindingMode: Immediate
allowVolumeExpansion: true
kubectl apply -f  efs-sc.yaml

NOTE:
  1)security group of  EFS must be add EFS port 2049 of EKS security group
  2) A Kubernetes service account configured with IRSA (IAM Role for Service Account), using the AWS-managed policy AmazonEFSCSIDriverPolicy, with permissions to create the aws-efs-csi-driver add-on
3) One EFS file system created in the same VPC as the EKS cluster:
 4)   Security group inbound rule allowing traffic on port 2049 from the EKS cluster security group.
5)	Dynamic access point creation in EFS should be allowed from EKS.
S3 ACCESS
1)	S3 bucket connectivity should be enabled for the EKS cluster
RDS CONNECTIVITY 
1)	An RDS PostgreSQL database configured to allow connectivity from the EKS cluster:
2)	The RDS security group should permit access from the EKS cluster security group.
3)	The RDS instance should reside in the same VPC as the EKS cluster.


Load Balancer
Application Load Balancer required with Target port 8080 to access the application 
AWS simple email service
IAM user with Amazon SES and credential management rights
Amazon SES configured in a same  region as CVAT
one verified sender identity (email or domain) in SES that will be used as DEFAULT_FROM_EMAIL / SERVER_EMAIL.
SES SMTP credentials (SMTP username and password) generated for that region.
SES SMTP endpoint and port
EKS nodes must allow outbound traffic on: TCP 587 (SMTP TLS) or 465 (SMTP SSL)

